# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Covers both client/ and server/ — one root .gitignore rather than a
# per-folder one, since every pattern below is one that should apply no
# matter which side of the repo it appears under (client/node_modules today,
# server/node_modules the day server/ gets real dependencies).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Dependencies ──
# Installed by `npm install`/`npm ci` from package-lock.json, never
# hand-edited, and enormous (client/node_modules alone was 20,955 files,
# ~190MB of this repo's .git history before this file existed). Committing it
# means every clone pays that cost, every dependency bump is a
# multi-thousand-file diff (a 7-package `npm uninstall` touched thousands of
# tracked files the day this was fixed), and none of it is a reviewable diff
# anyway. Keep the lockfile committed; drop the tree it generates. Matches at
# any depth, so this also covers server/node_modules once server/ has any.
node_modules/
jspm_packages/

# ── Environment files ──
# May hold real credentials depending on what a future .env adds — even
# where today's client/.env values are low-sensitivity, secrets shouldn't be
# trained into the habit of going through git. Applies equally to a future
# server/.env. The matching *.env.example (client/.env.example today) stays
# committed as the template documenting which variables exist.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.env.development.local
.env.test.local
.env.production.local

# ── Build output ──
# General rule: build output is generated, not authored, so it doesn't
# belong in git. client/dist/ is the deliberate, documented exception — see
# the comment in client/vite.config.js — because this site is deployed by
# copying that built output directly, with no build step on the server. If
# server/ ever gets a compiled-output directory (a `build/` or `out/` from a
# TypeScript or bundler setup), it should be ignored the normal way; add its
# name here explicitly rather than a repo-wide `dist/` that would also catch
# client's intentionally-tracked one.
server/dist/
server/build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── Logs, caches, and coverage ──
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
*.log
.npm/
.eslintcache
.cache/
coverage/
.nyc_output/

# ── Editor and OS cruft ──
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
*.swp
*~
